ROOT CAUSES (all reproduced locally from the checked-out source)
1. Manifest producer failure (collector rc=2). The "Write run manifest"
   step built JSON exclusively with `jq -n ... > ci-parts/manifest.json`.
   The `>` redirect creates the file BEFORE jq runs; with jq unavailable
   the step fails (rc=127) and leaves a ZERO-BYTE manifest.json behind,
   while ci-parts/download-diagnostics.txt is already written (matching the
   recorded evidence: five successful downloads recorded, evidence tree
   present). The collector guard `[ -x bin/ci-log-collector ] && [ -s
   ci-parts/manifest.json ]` then correctly refuses (recorded rc=2), and
   the emergency fallback reports "MANIFEST MISSING OR EMPTY" with
   environment-derived results and coverage UNVERIFIED. Deterministic local
   reproduction (scripts/repro-manifest-failure.sh, output
   ci-logs/repro-run-42376176493/repro.log): old writer + jq-less PATH ->
   step rc=127, download diagnostics present, manifest 0 bytes; guard -> 2.
   REPRO 3: collector binary vs empty/missing/invalid manifest -> rc=2 with
   distinct HARD_ERROR classes. REPRO 4 (repaired path): write-manifest.py
   -> valid manifest -> collector runs -> report written, coverage
   INCOMPLETE (windows artifact missing, as in the run), rc=1 fail-closed.
   Hosted raw logs were NOT RETRIEVED, so this remains a deterministic
   reproduction of the recorded signature, not an observation of the
   hosted step itself.
2. UCRT64 identity gate false negative. Verify-UCRT64.ps1 and the
   build-windows.ps1 desktop-env-check gate located `_mingw.h` via
   `gcc -print-file-name=include` — GCC's PRIVATE internal include
   directory (ucrt64\lib\gcc\x86_64-w64-mingw32\<ver>\include). The
   mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-headers package installs _mingw.h at the UCRT64
   TARGET header root <msys2>\ucrt64\include\_mingw.h (confirmed against
   the official MSYS2 package inventory cited in the run evidence). The
   run's windows-compiler.log proved a healthy UCRT64 toolchain (gcc path,
   target triple, _UCRT after <stdio.h>, UCRT64 pkgconf) and failed only
   on the wrong header path. Repaired: header checked at the target root
   derived from the compiler location; private dir kept as a clearly
   labeled diagnostic; pacman -Q (gcc/headers/crt/pkgconf) and
   pacman -Qo /ucrt64/include/_mingw.h ownership verification added;
   separate compile/link probe with PE MZ validation added. Runtime
   fixtures (pwsh, stub toolchain): 10/10 PASS including the exact
   false-negative shape.
3. GUI smoke false positive. build-linux.sh set WINDOW_MAPPED=1 solely
   because the title appeared in `xwininfo -root -tree` and "stayed
   mapped" by re-querying the XID only — the recorded window-stats.txt
   said `Map State: IsUnMapped`. Repaired: dedicated
   scripts/ci/x11-window-mapstate.sh requires xwininfo's Map State to be
   IsViewable (case-insensitive: xwininfo prints "IsUnMapped", Xlib's
   constant is IsUnmapped — both accepted, unknown values fail); map state
   is re-queried on every observation poll and captured at readiness,
   after observation and before shutdown; geometry must be plausible;
   Xvfb identity now comes from the package database (dpkg-query), never
   from the unsupported `-version` option; test-only ALSA null-PCM config
   scoped to the app process; ALSA messages classified as a structured
   environment limitation (never suppressed, panic/fatal still fails).
   End-to-end proof on this host: real app under real Xvfb — window
   0x400020 "SHEYTAN Music Visualizer", Map State: IsViewable at all
   three checkpoints (1440x860), graceful SIGTERM close rc=0, audio
   classification clean under the null PCM config.
4. Collector observability gap. "Run collector" steps did not capture the
   collector's stdout/stderr into ci-logs/collector-run.log and the
   fallback could not distinguish missing vs empty vs invalid manifest.
   Repaired: verbatim capture with the original rc preserved (no pipeline
   masking), command/cwd/absolute-manifest/byte-count/UTC window recorded,
   precise BLOCKED diagnosis branch, and the Go binary now emits distinct
   HARD_ERROR manifest-missing / manifest-empty / manifest-invalid prefixes
   (numeric 0/1/2/3 contract unchanged and documented).

